IMPORTANT REVIEW RULES
- Do not claim a vulnerability without evidence from the code.
- Do not claim a memory leak unless object retention/lifecycle supports it.
- Distinguish theoretical risk from exploitable vulnerability.
- Distinguish performance issue from scalability issue.
- Distinguish high allocation from memory leak.
- Distinguish duplicate delivery from duplicate business processing.
- Do not assume Kafka provides exactly-once business processing.
- Do not assume Kubernetes restart automatically makes processing safe.
- Do not assume multiple DCs are safe simply because the application is stateless.
- Do not recommend distributed locks without explaining why they are required.
- Prefer database constraints and idempotency where appropriate.
- Never recommend disabling security controls merely to resolve a performance issue.
- For financial operations, always evaluate idempotency, concurrency,
  transaction boundaries, retry behavior, and reconciliation.
- Provide evidence from the actual source code for each significant finding.
- Prioritize issues by actual production/business impact rather than number
  of static-analysis violations.

Recommended review output hierarchy

For your .NET ACH / payment platform, I would have the AI produce findings in this priority order:

P0 — Financial correctness / exploitable security

Could duplicate, lose, corrupt, or unauthorizedly alter a financial transaction.

P1 — Production correctness

Race conditions, inconsistent state, deadlocks, lost messages, unsafe recovery.

P2 — Resilience

Multi-instance, multi-DC, Kafka/MQ, database, storage, Kubernetes failure behavior.

P3 — Scalability/resource

Memory, CPU, ThreadPool, DB connections, sockets, file handles, queue growth.

P4 — Maintainability/changeability

Complexity, coupling, duplication, technical debt, architectural improvements.

This ordering prevents an AI reviewer from producing a report dominated by minor code-quality issues while missing something much more important such as two Kubernetes pods concurrently reserving the same funds, an Outbox race, a DbContext shared across threads, an unbounded file-processing queue, or a retry that can execute a financial transaction twice.
